In article <1991Sep23.032704.3829@panix.com>, jhawk@panix.com (John Hawkinson) writes...
>	a) Namely, how do you get the Mail delivery bit to work. [on VMS]
> [...] #version does say that it's compiled with Mail.

     In 3.0, compiling with MAIL #defined just enables broadcast trapping.
If a "new mail" message is sent to your screen, it will be captured and then
delivered as a scroll by a mail daemon.  As a matter of fact, _any_ broadcast
will be delivered as a scroll of new mail.  Reading the scroll just gives
the text of the broadcast, not the mail (or whatever) that the broadcast
was announcing.  You can spawn a subprocess (using '!') to read mail.

     If nethack.exe is built without MAIL being defined, then broadcasts
will just go straight to your screen and disrupt the display.  If you set
your terminal to disable broadcasts, then you won't get any scrolls of
new mail (or suffer screen disruption if MAIL wasn't defined).

>	b) Is there any sysadmin documentation other than Install.VMS?

     No, but the Unix man page is included with the source distribution in
file "auxil/nethack.6" or something close to that.  With a bit of formatting,
it can be used.  Most of the information is relevant to the VMS port.

     DCL converts unquoted command-line text into uppercase, then the
C Run-Time Library code that processes the command-line into argc and
argv[] for C's main() turns right around and converts unquoted text into
lowercase.  Therefore, if you want upper- or mixed-case text on the
command line, you need quotes.  For example,
 |$ nethack -u "Conan-B"
to play a barbarian (that's from -B) named "Conan" (-u).  If you want to
figure out which other command switches are available, read the Unix man
page or the parsing code in source file vms/vmsmain.c.  All the character
classes are available using -A, -B, -K, -W and so forth.  If you don't
use -u, then your character's name will match for username, unless you've
got it set to something else in NETHACKOPTIONS.

